        "info": "In Arabic, the infinitive form is known as \"المصدر\" (al-masdar), and it represents the base or root form of a verb. Unlike in English or some other languages, the Arabic infinitive is not marked by a specific suffix, and it remains constant throughout verb conjugation.\n\nHere are some key points about the Arabic infinitive:\n\nRoot Structure:\n\nArabic verbs typically consist of a root of three consonants (triliteral root). The infinitive reflects this root structure and provides the essential meaning of the verb.\nNo Specific Suffix:\n\nUnlike some languages where infinitives might end in -ing or -ar, Arabic infinitives do not have a specific suffix. The root itself serves as the basis for conjugating verbs into various tenses and forms.\nUsage in Verb Conjugation:\n\nWhen conjugating Arabic verbs, the infinitive is an essential reference point. Different verb forms are derived by adding specific prefixes, suffixes, or internal vowel changes to the infinitive.\nExample:\n\nFor the triliteral root K-T-B (كتب), the infinitive form is كتابة (kitābah), which means \"writing.\" This infinitive serves as the basis for constructing various verb forms.\nExample of Conjugation:\nInfinitive: كتابة (kitābah) - writing\nPresent Tense: أكتب (aktub) - I write\nPast Tense: كتبت (katabtu) - I wrote\nFuture Tense: سأكتب (sa'aktub) - I will write\nUnderstanding the infinitive form is fundamental for learning how to conjugate Arabic verbs and express different aspects, such as tense, mood, and voice.",
